dgp, sched, gate, domain, locking, sys, build: nucleus entity, dispatch-layer DGP chokepoint, RT mutex PI, signal framework, scheduler policy syscalls, lazy rights revocation, zinit initcall system, Perl configure

    This is the largest single change set since the original DGP commit
    (f03989e).  It introduces a real nucleus domain, completed DGP
    authorization at the dispatch layer, the rest of the Phase-4 SMP/
    NUMA scheduler hardening, the locking/PI bridge, the signal
    framework, scheduler-policy syscalls, lazy rights revocation, a
    zinit initcall system, and a Perl configure tool.  The tree builds
    cleanly (cl+lld, freestanding C++23) and all 58 user tests pass.

    * Two-layer model formally documented in the isolation_rights
      doxygen header: coarse domain-class authority (isolation_rights
      stored on domain::granted_rights, enforced by dgp_authorize)
      vs fine per-object capability rights (cap_rights packed in
      cap_token, enforced by cap_validate).  The two are not
      interchangeable and not duplicates — they cooperate.  The doc
      block includes a per-auth-level table showing where each layer
      is checked.

    * dispatch_table::validate_call now runs dgp_authorize for *every*
      registered op_kind, not only io/signal/gate_entry as before.
      This is defense-in-depth at the dispatch layer so a handler bug
      cannot accidentally bypass the DGP rights model.

    * dispatch_entry_meta carries an op_kind field, populated at
      registration time by gate_registration::make_op.  The dispatch
      layer uses it to derive the correct dgp_authorize invocation.

    * New target_domain_for_op() resolves the DGP target domain from
      the syscall arguments for cross-domain ops (domain_query,
      signal_send, mem_share, portal_create/destroy, domain_destroy,
      strand_create).  Domain id 0 resolves to the nucleus domain.

    * New metadata_for_op() populates dgp_authorization_metadata
      (target_va_start/end, gate_id) from syscall arguments for the
      ops that the DGP invariant requires: VA-bounded memory ops
      (mem_map, mem_unmap, mem_protect, mem_free, mem_share,
      portal_create) and gate-id carrying ops (gate_entry,
      io_irq_bind).  validate_op_metadata in dgp_authorize.cxxm is
      no longer dead code — the metadata is now actually populated
      and the VA/gate-id invariants it enforces become real gates.

    * All 8 nc_* handler families now share the dispatch-layer
      chokepoint on the way in.  Handler-side dgp_authorize calls
      remain in place as belt-and-suspenders against run-time
      handler-level regressions.

    * operation_kind enum renumbered into contiguous groups:
      domain_lifecycle (1-6), gate (7-9), portal (10-11), io (12-15),
      signal (16-22), mem (23-28), cap (29-32), sys (33-39),
      scheduling-policy (40-42).  required_rights_for_op and
      validate_op_metadata cases cover every operation_kind
      exhaustively; no -Wswitch errors.

    * cap_revoke added to cap_op (cap_op is now acquire/release/
      validate/revoke).  cap_revoke bumps the underlying object's
      generation counter, invalidating all outstanding tokens
      efficiently.  DGP-gated behind delegate|lifecycle.

    The kernel itself was previously a "blob of code with no face":
    domain_id 0 was reserved-as-invalid and domain_find(0) returned
    null, current_domain_ptr() was null pre-first-user-domain, printk
    had to fall back to a "nucleus" string literal, and the DGP
    cross-domain target path had no nucleus domain to authorize
    against.

    A new domain_init_nucleus() runs immediately after domain_init()
    and permanently reserves g_domain_table slot 0:
      - name is "foundation/core.zxfoundation.nucleus" (literal
        lifetime == kernel lifetime)
      - granted_rights = isolation_rights::all
      - storage_key = SKEY_NUCLEUS (architecture-reserved master key 0)
      - asce / root_phys seeded from the running CR1 (read via the
        public arch::s390x::cpu::ctlreg::ctl_store<1,1>())
      - lifecycle_state := active (set once, never transitions)
      - primary_strand fields initialised to safe idle defaults
        (ksp/kstack_top zero, substate=idle, owner=self, flags=idle);
        nucleus is not context-switched into — the kernel is already
        running on its own stack — but the fields are now non-bogus
        so scheduler introspection paths see a real object instead of
        uninitialised memory.
      - hard-forbid: domain_destroy refuses id 0 and any domain
        carrying domain_flags::nucleus, unconditionally.

    domain_nucleus() returns the slot-0 pointer; current_domain_or_
    nucleus() returns current_domain_ptr() or falls back to
    domain_nucleus() when no domain is attached to the running CPU.
    This eliminates the "null current domain" case throughout the
    boot path.

    Per-CPU idle strands now have owner == domain_nucleus() rather
    than nullptr — every strand in the system has a real domain
    owner. sched_init's BSP and AP bringup paths bind lowcore's
    current_domain to domain_nucleus() so the kernel-idle context
    runs "as nucleus" until the first user domain is scheduled, and
    svc/pgm/mcck trap handlers see a real caller domain instead of
    having to bail on null.

    domain_query(0) — historically special-cased in the handler —
    now resolves against the nucleus domain through the normal
    target_domain_for_op lookup, fixing an audit regression exposed
    by the new dispatch-layer DGP check.

    domain_flags::nucleus is architecturally unacquirable: domain_
    create() unconditionally initialises new domains with
    domain_flags::none.  The check in domain_destroy is defense-in-
    depth against internal corruption.

    * Module split sched.context (leaf: irqs/preempt/asserts) and
      sched.admit (high-level can_admit_domain).  No module SCCs;
      C++ module dependency graph verified clean.

    * strand.se (64 bytes) replaces the old sched + sched_rb pair.
      sched_entity carries vruntime, deadline, slice, lag, weight,
      rel_count and the EEVDF fair_node — sized exactly for the
      fair_tree rb-tree layout.

    * sched_avg (PELT, 72 bytes) embedded in strand_body; sched_avg_rq
      (72 bytes) embedded in run_queue::cfs_avg, mirroring Linux's
      cfs_rq::avg.

    * CBS replenishment (cbs_replen, <=96 bytes) embedded in strand_
      body; rt_bandwidth (<=96 bytes) per-CPU in run_queue::rt_bw.

    * CFS/RT/Deadline classes wired with enqueue/dequeue/pick_next/
      tick/check_preempt vtables.  Function-pointer bridge between
      sched_rt and sched.sched avoids the (sched.sched -> sched_class
      -> sched_rt) cycle: set_enqueue_accessor / set_dequeue_accessor
      / set_schedule_accessor, wired from init/main.cxxm after
      sched_init.

    * sched_balance given a set_rq_accessor() injection to avoid
      importing sched.sched; same pattern for rq_snap.

    * Per-CPU sched_domain_tree (SMT-MC-DIE-BOOK-NUMA) embedded in
      run_queue, built by sd::sched_domain_tree_build.

    * scheduler_tick, schedule(), softirq-driven load balancing,
      cpupri/cpudl, RT bandwidth throttling, deadline replenishment,
      PELT decay, nohz idle, SMT-aware capacity scaling.

    * BUG-003 fix: schedule()'s early-return path (next==prev) was
      leaving the re-picked strand with substate==runnable instead
      of substate==running.  Fixed in sched.cxxm; BUG_LIST.xml entry
      added.

    * locking/rt_mutex.cxxm — Linux rt_mutex_adjust_prio_chain-style
      chain-walk.  Lock-ordering rule: acquire the next mutex's
      m_pi_lock BEFORE releasing the previous mutex's m_pi_lock to
      avoid deadlocks during the chain walk.

    * DGP cross-domain gate for PI chain traversal: pi_may_cross()
      requires the owner domain to hold delegate and the waiter
      domain to hold enter.  MAX_PI_DEPTH = 16 bounds the walk.

    * sched_rt PI hooks (boost_prio / unboost_prio / task_wake_pi)
      driven through a fn-ptr bridge (set_enqueue_accessor and
      siblings) to break the sched.sched -> sched_class -> sched_rt
      cycle at the module level.  Wired from init/main.cxxm
      immediately after sched_init.

    * strand_body gains PI fields: blocked_on (void*), pi_waiters
      (lib::list_node), boosted_prio (u8), saved_class
      (sched_class_id), saved_prio (u8).

    * sgn_op: register_handler, poll, ack, send, mask_set, mask_get,
      wait.  All 7 dispatch cases implemented in nc_sgn.cxxm.

    * signal_send reads target domain id from a0, validates via
      dgp_authorize(signal_send) for the cross-domain DGP check,
      then enqueues to target domain's per-domain queue and fires
      ipi::send_reschedule to wake the target.

    * signal_wait checks pending in the caller's queue, falls through
      to schedule() if nothing deliverable.

    * scheduler(schedule(), in the prev_resched and next_owner paths)
      checks strand::has_pending() and sets need_resched so a queued
      signal is delivered promptly.

    * signal_deliver_all and block-aware signal_deliver_one
      delivered via domain/signal.cxxm.

    * gate_auth_level::signal added to the dispatch authorization
      model; dgp_rights_for returns enter|signal; validate_call
      enforces caller's signal right and target domain existence.

    * dom_op extended: create, destroy, exit, info, query,
      strand_create, strand_set_policy, strand_set_affinity,
      strand_query_state (9 ops total).

    * strand_set_policy reads a sched_policy from user VA and re-
      admits it via strand_admit_policy.  strand_set_affinity sets
      sched_policy.allowed_cpus.bits.  strand_query_state returns a
      packed u64 encoding (substate, class, on_rq, prio, cpu) with no
      struct ABI — consistent with the existing nc_domain_query
      pattern.

    All handlers wired through gate_registration.cxxm with the
    appropriate gate_auth_level and operation_kind metadata.

    * cap_op extended to 4 ops (acquire, release, validate, revoke).
    * nc_cap_revoke handler implemented; delegate|lifecycle-gated.
    * cap_revoke() bumps the object generation, invalidating all
      prior outstanding tokens atomically.

    * dgp::revoke_domain_rights() clears bits from granted_rights
      and ORs them into domain::pending_rights_revoke_raw, bumping
      revocation_epoch.  No cross-CPU IPI — propagation is lazy.

    * schedule() checks pending_rights_revoke_raw on each entry and
      force-dequeues the affected strand when revocation is pending,
      so a domain whose rights have been revoked cannot continue to
      execute.

    * scheduler_tick sweeps the current strand's domain, allowing
      pending revocations to flush on each CPU.

    * printk() now prints the calling domain's name in the log
      prefix: format is now
        ZXF!-[(SSSS).uuuuuu] [(type)NN] (T)@[domain] <message>
      so every line identifies which protection domain produced it.
      Pre-domain boot lines print "nucleus"; with the nucleus domain
      entity now real, this string now comes from the nucleus
      domain's name field rather than the literal fallback.

    * printk uses natural range-for loops, discard_value for
      intentional discard of nodiscard returns — no more (void)
      casts.

    * New subsystem: zxfoundation/sys/zinit.cxxm + zinit_types.cxxm.
      Provides a linker-section-driven initcall table (__zinit_start
      / __zinit_end) of zi_desc entries, each tagged with the
      subsystem name, init function pointer, and an action mask
      (silent/quiet/verbose).

    * Core subsystems register their boot-time initialisation
      functions through zinit descriptors; zinit::execute iterates
      the section and runs them in declaration order, enforcing the
      ordered init contract that was previously implicit in main.cxxm's
      call-list.

    * Replaced configure.cmake with configure.pl — a Perl-driven
      configuration helper that derives compiler selection (clang/
      gcc), opt level (0/1/2/3/s/z), debug-sym level, target march
      (z13..z16), DASD serial, build directory, generated toolchain
      file, and a "skip safeguard checks" flag.  Defaults are picked
      from a single hash table; override via env vars or command-line
      --KEY=VALUE pairs.  Generates build/toolchain.generated.cmake
      and build/config.generated.cmake.  Updates the run launcher
      scripts accordingly.

    * cmake/zx-discovery.cmake and toolchain-validate.cmake
      updated to consume the generated config.  New flags.cmake
      clauses enforce freestanding + no-rtti + no-exceptions + no-
      unwind-tables.

    * Linker script and CMakeFiles updated for zinit section
      markers (__zinit_start / __zinit_end).

    * .gitignore adjusted to ignore the generated toolchain files
      and the new build/ directory layout.

    * lib::list_head gains insert_before() for non-tail list
      insertions; required by rt_mutex pi_waiters ordering.

    * lib::string_copy converted from a byte-by-byte loop to a
      memcpy-based bulk copy that uses the architecture's s390x mvc
      instruction.  Two off-by-one bugs in a commented-out "fast"
      alternative were caught during the audit; correct invariant is
      n = min(src.size(), cap - 1), followed by memcpy of n bytes
      and a single NUL terminator at dest[n].

    * softirq split into a types module (softirq_types) and the
      implementation module (softirq) to break module-dependency
      cycles for users like sched_softirq.

    * variant polish: improved visitor interface; expected::transform
      added.  rbtree minor stability fixes.

    user/init/init.S extended to 58 tests, exercising every new
    syscall (sgn_send, sgn_mask_set, sgn_wait, dom_strand_create,
    dom_strand_set_policy, dom_strand_set_affinity, dom_strand_
    query_state, cap_revoke, mem operations, signal delivery cross-
    domain).  Test 37 (mem::share) was changed from "accept either"
    to an explicit negative test asserting ERR_SENTINEL — DGP
    correctly denies cross-domain share when the caller lacks the
    share right.  Test 3 (dom::query(0)) now resolves through the
    real nucleus domain entity rather than a handler-side
    special-case.

    * BUG-003 (Closed): schedule's next==prev early-return path was
      leaving the re-picked strand in substate==runnable.  Fixed;
      BUG_LIST.xml entry added.

